<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<activity>
  <title>Designing Fair Districts</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this activity, you will design district boundaries for a small state and use fairness measures to compare alternative plans.
      The exercise is intended to make the trade-offs in redistricting visible.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <title>Build Three Districts</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The map below divides a small state into nine precincts. Each precinct lists the number of voters who prefer Party A and Party B.
          Your task is to group precincts into three contiguous districts with roughly equal population.
        </p>

        <table>
          <title>Precinct Data</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>Precinct</cell>
              <cell>Party A</cell>
              <cell>Party B</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>120</cell>
              <cell>80</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>90</cell>
              <cell>110</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>3</cell>
              <cell>60</cell>
              <cell>140</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>4</cell>
              <cell>130</cell>
              <cell>70</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>100</cell>
              <cell>100</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>6</cell>
              <cell>80</cell>
              <cell>120</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>7</cell>
              <cell>150</cell>
              <cell>50</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>8</cell>
              <cell>110</cell>
              <cell>90</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>9</cell>
              <cell>70</cell>
              <cell>130</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Assume the precincts are arranged in a 3x3 grid with precincts 1-3 in the top row, 4-6 in the middle row, and 7-9 in the bottom row.
          Districts must be contiguous under this grid.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li><p>Propose one plan that gives Party A the largest number of districts without splitting precincts.</p></li>
          <li><p>Propose one plan that is more proportional to the statewide vote share.</p></li>
          <li><p>For each plan, compute the total population in each district and determine whether the plan satisfies equal population requirements.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute the district winners for each plan and compare how many seats each party would win.</p></li>
          <li><p>Explain how compactness and contiguity influence whether a plan appears fair, and whether these measures always prevent partisan advantage.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <title>Voting Rights and Representation</title>
      <statement>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Identify a plan that creates a majority-minority district if precinct 3, 6, and 9 are considered to have a protected group that is 60% of their population.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Discuss whether your plan protects the voting power of this group while still respecting compactness and contiguity.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Describe how a court might evaluate your plan under the Voting Rights Act and how mathematical measures can support that evaluation.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</activity>
